          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Jane\",\"addressee\":\"St John\",\"anchor_quote\":\"There is no dishonour, no breach of promise, no desertion in the case.\\nI am not under the slightest obligation to go to India, especially with\\nstrangers. With you I would have ventured much, because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you; but I am convinced that, go\\nwhen and with whom I would, I should not live long in that climate.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Jane directly avows loving St John, explicitly defining the feeling as sisterly rather than romantic. The surrounding references to admiration and trust reinforce that this is a considered presentation of her affectionate state.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The focal expression does not undertake marriage, travel, or missionary service. Jane uses it while expressly denying obligation and distinguishing her affection from the commitment St John seeks. Her earlier statement, “Yes, I will, as your assistant,” is a separate and contested offer; it does not convert this sisterly avowal into a promise. “With you I would have ventured much” is hypothetical and explanatory rather than a present undertaking.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I am not under the slightest obligation to go to India, especially with\\nstrangers.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"With you I would have ventured much, because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Yes, I will, as your assistant\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.95,\"reason\":\"Although the exchange is emotionally strained, the focal words occur within a deliberate argument explaining Jane’s position. They are not represented as an involuntary cry, reflex, or expressive discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I replied—\\n\\n“There is no dishonour, no breach of promise, no desertion in the case.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing affection, the expression has a moderate justificatory and conciliatory function: it explains why Jane would have trusted St John enough to venture much with him while also marking the relationship as sibling-like and rejecting his marital interpretation.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"With you I would have ventured much, because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I say again, I will be your\\ncurate, if you like, but never your wife.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"During a dispute about St John’s proposed marriage and missionary journey to India, Jane rejects his allegation that she is breaking a promise. She avows sisterly love as part of explaining her exceptional trust in him, while refusing marriage and questioning whether she should go to India at all.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Jane has separately offered to accompany St John as an assistant or sister, but he rejects that arrangement and insists that she must be his wife. In the focal turn she expressly denies any obligation, describes what she “would have ventured” with him, and raises the likelihood of dying in the climate. Thus the focal “I love you” affirms sisterly affection but neither creates nor renews the proposed undertaking. St John does not acknowledge the avowal affectionately; he answers by accusing her of fear and continues challenging her decision.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passages are recognisable as Charlotte Brontë’s Jane Eyre. The classification relies only on the supplied blocks.\",\"limitations\":\"The evidence establishes Jane’s represented sisterly affection and the immediate dispute clearly. It does not independently settle every possible term or duration of her earlier offer to serve as an assistant, but it does show that St John rejected that non-marital arrangement and that Jane denies being bound to go.\"}"
